{% endfor %}

Write for a reader who has to decide, quickly, whether an incoming question is something this knowledge base can help with. That decision is the paragraph's only job, so:

  - Name the concrete subjects covered — the things a user would ask about — rather than characterising the document ("a comprehensive guide to...").
  - Include the specific nouns a question would contain: the kinds of dates, fees, procedures, people, places and rules that appear. A reader matching a question against this paragraph needs those words to match against.
  - Prefer the corpus's own vocabulary over generic paraphrase.
  - State the boundaries where they are clear — who or what the material is about, and any period or population it applies to.
  - Do not invent coverage. If something is absent from the summaries, it is absent from the corpus.
{% if languages %}
  - Where the material uses more than one language, keep the key terms in {{ languages }}, so questions in either one have something to match.
{% endif %}

One paragraph. No preamble, no heading, no bullet points — return the paragraph itself and nothing else.
